<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Separable Differential Equations</title>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Solve the initial value problem:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = 2y^2</mrow>
          <mrow>y(-1) \amp = 2</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="28">
      <p>
        Separating variables and integrating:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \int y^{-2}\,dy \amp = \int 2\,dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            -\frac{1}{y} \amp = 2t + C
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        This is a good place to solve for the constant <m>C</m>, which
        we do using the fact that
        <m>y(-1) = 2</m>.  This means that
        <md>
          -\frac12 = 2(-1)+C
        </md>
        so <m>C = \frac{3}{2}</m>.  This means that
        <md>
          <mrow>
            -\frac1y \amp = 2t+\frac32
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            y \amp = -\frac{1}{2t+\frac32} = -\frac2{4t+3}
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Solve the initial value problem:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \frac{dy}{dt} \amp = -\frac{t}{y}
          </mrow>
          <mrow>y(0) \amp = 5</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="28">
      <p>
        Separating variables:
        <md>
          <mrow>
            y\frac{dy}{dt} \amp = -t
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \int y~dy \amp =-\int t~dt
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \frac12y^2 \amp =-\frac12t^2 + C
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            y^2 \amp =-t^2 + C
          </mrow>
        </md>.
        Since
        <m>y(0) = 5</m>, we have <m>C = 25</m>, which leaves us with
        <md>
          y = \sqrt{25 - t^2}
        </md>.
        Notice we take the positive square root since <m>y(0)=5\gt
        0</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Suppose that a cup of coffee is initially at
        a temperature of 105<m>^\circ</m> F and is
        placed in a 75<m>^\circ</m> F room.  Newton's
        law of cooling says that
        <md>
          \frac{dT}{dt} = -k(T-75),
        </md>
        where <m>k</m> is a constant of
        proportionality.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose you know that the coffee is cooling
          at one degree per minute when it is brought
          into the room.  Use this to determine the
          value of the constant <m>k</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          At <m>t=0</m>, <m>T=105</m> and
          <m>dT/dt = -1</m>, so
          <m>-1 = -k(105-75) = -30k</m>, giving
          <m>k = \frac{1}{30}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find all solutions to the differential
          equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Separating variables:
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac{1}{T-75}\frac{dT}{dt} \amp = -\frac1{30}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int\frac{1}{T-75}~dT \amp = -\int\frac1{30}~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \ln|T-75| \amp = -\frac1{30}t + C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              |T-75| \amp = e^Ce^{-t/30}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T-75 \amp = Ae^{-t/30}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              T \amp = 75 + Ae^{-t/30}
            </mrow>
          </md>
          for any constant <m>A</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What happens to all the solutions as
          <m>t\to\infty</m>?  Explain how this agrees
          with your intuition.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          As <m>t\to\infty</m>, <m>T\to 75</m>°F.
          The coffee cools to room temperature,
          as expected.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution with <m>T(0) = 105</m>.
          What is the temperature of the cup of
          coffee after 20 minutes?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>T(0) = 75 + A = 105</m> gives
          <m>A = 30</m>, so
          <m>T(t) = 75 + 30e^{-t/30}</m>.
          After 20 minutes, we have
          <md>
            T(20) = 75 + 30e^{-2/3} \approx 90.4^\circ
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take to cool to
          80<m>^\circ</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Setting <m>75 + 30e^{-t/30} = 80</m> leads to
          <md>
            e^{-t/30} = \frac{1}{6}
          </md>
          so <m>t = 30\ln 6 \approx 53.8</m> minutes.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Over time, a sample of a radioactive material
        will decay at a rate proportional to its mass.
        If we call <m>M(t)</m> the mass of the
        radioactive substance, we can express this
        fact by
        <md>
          \frac{dM}{dt}=-kM.
        </md>
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the general solution of the differential
          equation.  (Your solution will depend on the
          constant of proportionality <m>k</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          Separating variables gives
          <md>
            <mrow>
              \frac1M\frac{dM}{dt} \amp = -k
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \int \frac1M~dM \amp =-\int k~dt
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              \ln|M|\amp = -kt + C
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              |M|\amp = e^Ce^{-kt}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
              M \amp = Ae^{-kt}
            </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now find the solution where the initial mass
          is <m>M(0) = M_0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>M(0) = A = M_0</m>, so
          <m>M(t) = M_0 e^{-kt}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The half-life of a radioactive substance is
          the amount of time required for half the mass
          to decay.  Carbon-14 has a half-life of 5730
          years.  What is the value of <m>k</m> for
          Carbon-14?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>M_0 e^{-5730k} = \frac12M_0</m>, so
          <m>e^{-5730k} = \frac12</m> and
          <md>
            k = \frac{\ln 2}{5730} \approx 0.000121
          </md>
          per year.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If the mass of a Carbon-14 sample is
          initially 20 grams, how long does it take
          until there are only 5 grams left?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="28">
        <p>
          <m>20e^{-kt} = 5</m> gives
          <m>e^{-kt} = \frac{1}{4}</m>, so
          <md>
            t = \frac{\ln 4}{k} = 2 \cdot 5730
            = 11460
          </md>
          years.  This is exactly two half-lives since we start with
          20 grams and go to 10 and then 5 grams.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
